    "diff_hunk": "@@ -128958,15 +128958,17 @@ document.body.appendChild(text);\n     <code>p</code> element in button scope</span>, then <span>close a <code>p</code>\n     element</span>.</p>\n \n-    <p><span>Reconstruct the active formatting elements</span>, if any.</p>\n-\n     <p><span>Insert an HTML element</span> for the token.</p>\n \n     <p>Switch the tokenizer to the <span>PLAINTEXT state</span>.</p>\n \n-    <p class=\"note\">Once a start tag with the tag name \"plaintext\" has been seen, that will be the\n-    last token ever seen other than character tokens (and the end-of-file token), because there is\n-    no way to switch out of the <span>PLAINTEXT state</span>.</p>\n+    <p class=\"note\">Once a start tag with the tag name \"plaintext\" has been seen, all remaining\n+    tokens will be character tokens (and a final end-of-file token), because there is\n+    no way to switch the tokenizer out of the <span>PLAINTEXT state</span>. However, as the\n+    tree builder remains in its existing insertion mode, it may <span>Reconstruct the active\n+    formatting elements</span>, if any, while processing those character tokens. This means\n+    that it's possible for a <code id=\"parsing-main-inbody:the-plaintext-element\"><a\n+    href=\"#the-plaintext-element\">plaintext</a></code> element to contain child nodes.</p>",
